.TH DATETIME 1 "September 2026" "datetime 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
datetime, timestamp \- print or set the system date and time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B datetime
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fI+FORMAT\fR]
.br
.B datetime
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIMMDDhhmm\fR[[\fICC\fR]\fIYY\fR][\fI.ss\fR]]
.br
.B timestamp
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBdatetime\fR displays the current date and time in the given \fIFORMAT\fR,
or sets the system clock when \fB-s\fR / \fB--set\fR or a \fIMMDDhhmm\fR argument is given.
Without options it prints the local time as \fIYYYYMMDDhhmmss\fR.
.PP
\fBtimestamp\fR is the same binary invoked as \fBtimestamp\fR (symlink or copy):
it always prints UTC as \fIYYYYMMDDhhmmssZ\fR (compact seconds, trailing \fBZ\fR).
The same output is obtained with \fBdatetime --timestamp\fR.
.PP
The program is written in strict \fBC99\fR (\-std=c99, only \fIlibc\fR, no external
libraries) and exposes GNU \fBdate\fR compatible options.
.SH OPTIONS
Mandatory arguments to long options are mandatory for short options too.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " --date =\fISTRING\fR
display time described by \fISTRING\fR, not `now'. \fISTRING\fR may be:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB@\fR\fIEPOCH\fR[\fB.\fINANO\fR]  – seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00 UTC, e.g. @0, @2147483647, @1234567890.123456789
.IP \(bu 2
\fIYYYY-MM-DD\fR, \fIYYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS\fR, \fIYYYY/MM/DD\fR, \fIMM/DD/YY\fR, ISO \fBT\fR and RFC 2822 variants
.IP \(bu 2
\fInow\fR, \fItoday\fR, \fIyesterday\fR, \fItomorrow\fR
.IP \(bu 2
any string understood by GNU \fBdate -d\fR as fallback (e.g. \fInext Fri\fR, \fI+1 day\fR, \fI2 weeks ago\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
\fITZ="\fR\fIZone\fR\fB" \fR\fISTRING\fR – temporarily sets \fBTZ\fR for parsing (e.g. TZ="America/Los_Angeles" 09:00 next Fri)
.RE
.TP
.BR --debug
annotate the parsed date and warn about questionable usage on standard error.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " --file =\fIDATEFILE\fR
like \fB--date\fR once for each line of \fIDATEFILE\fR. If \fIDATEFILE\fR is \fB-\fR, read from standard input.
.TP
.BI \-I "[\fIFMT\fR]" ", " --iso-8601 "[\fB=\fIFMT\fR]"
output date/time in ISO 8601 format.
\fIFMT\fR=\fBdate\fR (default), \fBhours\fR, \fBminutes\fR, \fBseconds\fR, or \fBns\fR.
Example: 2006-08-14T02:34:56-06:00 (ns adds \fI.000000000\fR).
.TP
.B --resolution
output the available resolution of timestamps. Example: \fI0.000000001\fR (nanosecond).
.TP
.BR \-R ", " --rfc-email
output date and time in RFC 5322 format. Example: \fIMon, 14 Aug 2006 02:34:56 +0000\fR
.TP
.BI --rfc-3339= FMT
output date/time in RFC 3339 format. \fIFMT\fR=\fBdate\fR, \fBseconds\fR, or \fBns\fR.
Example: \fI2006-08-14 02:34:56-06:00\fR
.TP
.BR \-r ", " --reference =\fIFILE\fR
display the last modification time of \fIFILE\fR.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " --set =\fISTRING\fR
set time described by \fISTRING\fR (requires root; otherwise warns and prints what would be set).
.TP
.BR \-u ", " --utc ", " --universal
print or set Coordinated Universal Time (UTC).
.TP
.B --help
display this help and exit.
.TP
.B --version
output version information and exit (e.g. \fIdatetime 2.0.20260909083730Z\fR).
.SS "Legacy output formats (kept for compatibility)"
.TP
(default)
\fIYYYYMMDDhhmmss\fR
.TP
.BR \-hr ", " --human-readable
\fIYYYY-MM-DD hh:mm:ss\fR
.TP
.BR \-c ", " --compact ", " iso-basic
\fIYYYYMMDDThhmmss\fR (ISO 8601 basic)
.TP
.BR \-cd ", " --calendar-date
\fIYYYY-MM-DD\fR
.TP
.BR \-cdb ", " --calendar-date-base
\fIYYYYMMDD\fR
.TP
.BR \-od ", " --ordinal-date
\fIYYYY-DDD\fR (001..366)
.TP
.BR \-odb ", " --ordinal-date-base
\fIYYYYDDD\fR
.TP
.BR \-wd ", " --week-date
\fIYYYY-Www-D\fR (week 01..53, 1=Mon..7=Sun)
.TP
.BR \-wdb ", " --week-date-basic
\fIYYYYWwwD\fR
.TP
.B --timestamp
\fIYYYYMMDDhhmmssZ\fR (UTC, compact seconds for micro version)
.PP
All options that specify the date to display are mutually exclusive:
\fB--date\fR, \fB--file\fR, \fB--reference\fR, \fB--resolution\fR.
.SH FORMAT
\fIFORMAT\fR controls the output. Interpreted sequences are:
.TP
.B %%
a literal %
.TP
.B %a
locale's abbreviated weekday name (e.g., Sun)
.TP
.B %A
locale's full weekday name (e.g., Sunday)
.TP
.B %b
locale's abbreviated month name (e.g., Jan)
.TP
.B %B
locale's full month name (e.g., January)
.TP
.B %c
locale's date and time (e.g., Thu Mar 3 23:05:25 2005)
.TP
.B %C
century; like %Y, except omit last two digits (e.g., 20)
.TP
.B %d
day of month (e.g., 01)
.TP
.B %D
date; same as %m/%d/%y
.TP
.B %e
day of month, space padded; same as %_d
.TP
.B %F
full date; same as %+4Y-%m-%d
.TP
.B %g
last two digits of year of ISO week number (e.g., 00..99); see %G
.TP
.B %G
year of ISO week number; normally useful only with %V
.TP
.B %h
same as %b
.TP
.B %H
hour (00..23)
.TP
.B %I
hour (01..12)
.TP
.B %j
day of year (001..366)
.TP
.B %k
hour, space padded ( 0..23); same as %_H
.TP
.B %l
hour, space padded ( 1..12); same as %_I
.TP
.B %m
month (01..12)
.TP
.B %M
minute (00..59)
.TP
.B %n
a newline
.TP
.B %N
nanoseconds (000000000..999999999)
.TP
.B %p
locale's equivalent of either AM or PM; blank if not known
.TP
.B %P
like %p, but lower case
.TP
.B %q
quarter of year (1..4)
.TP
.B %r
locale's 12-hour clock time (e.g., 11:11:04 PM)
.TP
.B %R
24-hour hour and minute; same as %H:%M
.TP
.B %s
seconds since the Epoch (1970-01-01 00:00 UTC)
.TP
.B %S
second (00..60)
.TP
.B %t
a tab
.TP
.B %T
time; same as %H:%M:%S
.TP
.B %u
day of week (1..7); 1 is Monday
.TP
.B %U
week number of year, with Sunday as first day of week (00..53)
.TP
.B %V
ISO week number, with Monday as first day of week (01..53)
.TP
.B %w
day of week (0..6); 0 is Sunday
.TP
.B %W
week number of year, with Monday as first day of week (00..53)
.TP
.B %x
locale's date representation
.TP
.B %X
locale's time representation
.TP
.B %y
last two digits of year (00..99)
.TP
.B %Y
year
.TP
.B %z
+hhmm numeric time zone (e.g., -0400)
.TP
.B %:z
+hh:mm numeric time zone (e.g., -04:00)
.TP
.B %::z
+hh:mm:ss numeric time zone (e.g., -04:00:00)
.TP
.B %:::z
numeric time zone with : to necessary precision (e.g., -04, +05:30)
.TP
.B %Z
alphabetic time zone abbreviation (e.g., EDT)
.PP
By default, date pads numeric fields with zeroes.
The following optional flags may follow `%`:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB-\fR (hyphen) do not pad the field
.IP \(bu 2
\fB_\fR (underscore) pad with spaces
.IP \(bu 2
\fB0\fR (zero) pad with zeros
.IP \(bu 2
\fB+\fR pad with zeros, and put `+' before future years with >4 digits
.IP \(bu 2
\fB^\fR use upper case if possible
.IP \(bu 2
\fB#\fR use opposite case if possible
.RE
.PP
After any flags comes an optional field width, as a decimal number;
then an optional modifier, which is either \fBE\fR to use the locale's alternate
representations if available, or \fBO\fR to use the locale's alternate numeric
symbols if available.
.SH EXAMPLES
Convert seconds since the Epoch to a date:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ date --date='@2147483647'
$ datetime -d '@2147483647' -u
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show the time on the west coast of the US:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ TZ='America/Los_Angeles' date
$ datetime -d 'TZ="America/Los_Angeles" now'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Legacy and GNU examples:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ datetime                        # 20260909103730
$ datetime -hr                    # 2026-09-09 10:37:30
$ datetime -c                     # 20260909T103730
$ datetime --iso-8601=ns          # 2026-09-09T10:37:30.123456789+02:00
$ datetime -R                     # Wed, 09 Sep 2026 10:37:30 +0200
$ datetime --rfc-3339=seconds     # 2026-09-09 10:37:30+02:00
$ datetime -r Makefile +"%F %T"   # file mtime
$ datetime -f dates.txt +"%F"
$ printf "2020-01-02\\n" | datetime -f - +"%F"
$ datetime -u +"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %Z"
$ datetime --debug -d "2020-01-02"
$ timestamp                       # 20260909083730Z (UTC)
$ datetime --timestamp
.fi
.RE
.PP
Timestamp and microversion:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ~/sbin/timestamp                # 20260909083730Z
$ TS=$(~/sbin/timestamp); echo "2.0.$TS" # 2.0.20260909083730Z
$ make                            # regenerates version.h with fresh BUILD
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.I date_english.hlp
\- English help, ESC-filtered, 134 lines
.br
.I date_german.hlp
\- German help, ESC-filtered, 127 lines
.br
.I version.h
\- generated header with \fBVERSION_BUILD\fR (YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ)
.br
.I ~/sbin/datetime
\- installed binary (via \fBmake install\fR)
.br
.I ~/sbin/timestamp
\- same binary installed as \fBtimestamp\fR
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR date (1),
.BR strftime (3),
.BR tzselect (1)
.br
Full documentation in \fBREADME.md:1\fR and \fBdate_english.hlp:1\fR.
.SH AUTHOR
Written for the \fBdatetime\fR project (C99, 2.0). Uses only \fIlibc\fR.
